# The Weight of a Feather

Marisol had always thought her grandmother was exaggerating when she said that love could be felt in ordinary things. "Abuela, that's just poetry," Marisol would say, rolling her eyes as a teenager. "Love is a feeling, not something you can hold in your hands."

Her grandmother would just smile and continue whatever she was doing—usually embroidering, her needle pulling bright threads through fabric like she was stitching sunlight into cloth.

Now, twenty years later, Marisol stood in her grandmother's empty bedroom, a cardboard box at her feet. Abuela had passed away two weeks ago, and the family had gathered to sort through her belongings. Marisol had volunteered for this room. She wasn't sure why, except that it felt like the room still held her grandmother's warmth, like a stone holds the memory of the sun.

On the bed sat a pillow—plain white cotton, slightly yellowed with age, nothing remarkable at all. But Marisol recognized it instantly. It was the pillow her grandmother had made for her when Marisol was seven and terrified of thunderstorms.

*"Mi amor,"* her grandmother had said back then, sitting on the edge of Marisol's small bed while rain hammered the windows, *"this pillow is special. Inside, I've sewn a piece of cloud. When you're scared, hold it close, and remember that I am holding you too."*

Seven-year-old Marisol had believed it completely. She'd squeezed that pillow through every storm, every nightmare, every fever. She'd felt the cloud inside—soft and impossible, carrying her grandmother's promise.

Marisol picked up the pillow now, her fingers finding the familiar lump in its center. She pressed it, and something rustled. Curious, she found the small seam her grandmother had left and gently pulled it open.

Inside was a folded piece of paper, soft as cloth from age. Marisol unfolded it with trembling hands.

*Marisol, mi corazón,*

*There is no cloud in this pillow. You knew that, I think, even at seven. But do you know what is inside it? Every night you slept with it, I said a prayer for you. Every storm, I sat outside your door until you fell asleep. The pillow was never magic. It was just something your hands could hold while my love held you.*

*Love is like this, mija. It hides inside ordinary things. A meal cooked. A phone call made. A pillow sewn. We cannot see love itself, only the things it travels in. So look for it in everything. It is always there, hiding like a cloud in a pillow, waiting to be found.*

*Con todo mi amor,*
*Abuela*

Marisol sat down on the bed, the letter in her lap, and laughed and cried at the same time. Her grandmother hadn't been exaggerating at all. Love really could be held in your hands—you just had to know what you were holding.

She brought the pillow to her chest, the way she had as a child, and could have sworn it felt lighter than air—like holding a piece of cloud after all.

That night, Marisol placed the pillow on her own bed at home. And when her daughter came in, scared of a thunderstorm rolling over the hills, Marisol opened her arms and knew exactly what to say.

"Come here, mi amor. I have something to show you about clouds." [end of text]


